    "text_2001972900_copy_1019187017": {
      "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
      "jcr:createdBy": "user-c26bb7a766@example.invalid",
      "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "user-c26bb7a766@example.invalid",
      "jcr:created": "Thu Jul 15 2021 17:14:25 GMT+0000",
      "text": "<p>Mandiblular distraction is a surgical procedure that can increase the length of the jaw. Dr. Lung began the distraction using Stryker’s <a href=\"https://cmf.stryker.com/products/pediatric-mandibular-distractor\">Pediatric Mandibular Distractor (PMD) with anti-reverse mechanism</a>, a tool intended to help elongate Philip’s lower jaw. By fixating the linear distractor to Philip’s mandible and slowly turning it half a millimeter to a millimeter a day, Dr. Lung was able to open up Philip’s airway. Similar to palate expanders used in orthodontics, a small end of the distractor is accessible by the parent, who uses a special screwdriver at a specified time to make the exact measured turns. Dr. Lung was proud to report that Philip’s surgery was a success.</p>\n<p>“In Philip’s case, it was quite interesting because not only did I want to open up his airway but I also wanted to improve his (facial) symmetry. Before surgery, we developed a virtual surgical plan so I knew before going in to surgery what we could achieve. We provided a surgical simulation video to Philip’s parents so they could actually see what was going to happen before we did the surgery. They were extremely impressed,” says Dr. Lung.</p>\n<p>After reviewing the surgical plan and asking questions, Wilhelmine was ready to take on Goldenhar syndrome with her son. She felt hopeful that the surgical procedure could improve his facial structure and most importantly, his quality of life.</p>\n",
      "jcr:lastModified": "Thu Jul 15 2021 17:37:31 GMT+0000",
      "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/text",
